1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,919
The app crashes. A wall of text scrolls past. Most people see noise, and start guessing.

2
00:00:06,469 --> 00:00:08,375
But a stack trace is not noise.

3
00:00:08,375 --> 00:00:09,112
It is a map,

4
00:00:09,112 --> 00:00:10,957
written by the program itself,

5
00:00:10,957 --> 00:00:12,617
of exactly what went wrong,

6
00:00:12,617 --> 00:00:13,231
and where.

7
00:00:13,781 --> 00:00:19,136
Today you learn to read one in ten seconds. Then you find the same failure in a log file.

8
00:00:19,686 --> 00:00:23,739
No guessing. Four questions, and the answers are already on the screen.

9
00:00:25,789 --> 00:00:28,584
Four questions. Every stack trace answers them.

10
00:00:29,134 --> 00:00:33,123
One. What happened? The first line. The exception name, then the message.

11
00:00:33,673 --> 00:00:33,917
Two.

12
00:00:33,917 --> 00:00:34,283
Where?

13
00:00:34,283 --> 00:00:36,112
The lines that start with: at.

14
00:00:36,112 --> 00:00:38,125
The top one is where it happened.

15
00:00:38,125 --> 00:00:40,137
Each line below is who called it.

16
00:00:40,687 --> 00:00:41,060
Three.

17
00:00:41,060 --> 00:00:42,243
Which code is mine?

18
00:00:42,243 --> 00:00:43,736
Skip the Java internals.

19
00:00:43,736 --> 00:00:46,660
Find the first line with your own package name.

20
00:00:47,210 --> 00:00:51,456
Four. Why, really? Look for: Caused by. The last one is the root cause.

21
00:00:52,006 --> 00:00:55,675
Then you confirm it in the log: a time, a level, and a message.

22
00:00:57,725 --> 00:01:00,712
Go to the shop folder. Type: CD, shop.

23
00:01:01,262 --> 00:01:05,912
Compile the program. Type: java C, dash D, out, then the source path.

24
00:01:06,462 --> 00:01:11,348
Run it. Type: java, dash C P, out, com dot cloud cart dot checkout.

25
00:01:11,898 --> 00:01:12,602
First line.

26
00:01:12,602 --> 00:01:15,418
Illegal state exception: cannot price order.

27
00:01:15,418 --> 00:01:16,826
That is what happened,

28
00:01:16,826 --> 00:01:18,682
in words the developer chose.

29
00:01:19,232 --> 00:01:20,863
Now the Caused by line.

30
00:01:20,863 --> 00:01:22,565
Number format exception,

31
00:01:22,565 --> 00:01:24,338
for input string: x five.

32
00:01:24,338 --> 00:01:25,969
That is the root cause.

33
00:01:25,969 --> 00:01:27,813
A price arrived as x five,

34
00:01:27,813 --> 00:01:29,728
and x five is not a number.

35
00:01:30,278 --> 00:01:30,880
Below it,

36
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:33,824
the java dot base lines are Java's own code.

37
00:01:33,824 --> 00:01:34,493
Skip them.

38
00:01:34,493 --> 00:01:38,174
The first line with our package is: Checkout dot price,

39
00:01:38,174 --> 00:01:38,843
line five.

40
00:01:38,843 --> 00:01:42,523
That is where our code gave the bad value to parse int.

41
00:01:43,073 --> 00:01:47,190
Check the exit code. Type: echo, dollar sign, question mark.

42
00:01:47,740 --> 00:01:52,242
One. Not zero. The program failed, and a script or a pipeline can see that.

43
00:01:52,792 --> 00:01:54,859
Long traces need scanning.

44
00:01:54,859 --> 00:01:55,734
Type: java,

45
00:01:55,734 --> 00:01:56,450
dash C P,

46
00:01:56,450 --> 00:01:56,768
out,

47
00:01:56,768 --> 00:01:59,313
com dot cloud cart dot checkout,

48
00:01:59,313 --> 00:01:59,790
space,

49
00:01:59,790 --> 00:02:00,108
two,

50
00:02:00,108 --> 00:02:01,142
greater than,

51
00:02:01,142 --> 00:02:01,937
ampersand,

52
00:02:01,937 --> 00:02:02,255
one,

53
00:02:02,255 --> 00:02:02,653
pipe,

54
00:02:02,653 --> 00:02:03,051
grep,

55
00:02:03,051 --> 00:02:03,607
dash N,

56
00:02:03,607 --> 00:02:04,085
quote,

57
00:02:04,085 --> 00:02:04,880
Caused by,

58
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:05,357
quote.

59
00:02:05,907 --> 00:02:07,990
Stack traces go to standard error.

60
00:02:07,990 --> 00:02:11,421
Two greater than ampersand one sends them down the pipe.

61
00:02:11,421 --> 00:02:14,178
Grep finds the root cause line in one second,

62
00:02:14,178 --> 00:02:15,464
with its line number.

63
00:02:17,514 --> 00:02:20,501
Go to the shop folder. Type: CD, shop.

64
00:02:21,051 --> 00:02:25,936
Look at the newest log lines. Type: tail, dash N, five, app dot log.

65
00:02:26,486 --> 00:02:27,780
Each line: a time,

66
00:02:27,780 --> 00:02:28,356
a level,

67
00:02:28,356 --> 00:02:29,075
a message.

68
00:02:29,075 --> 00:02:30,153
Info is normal.

69
00:02:30,153 --> 00:02:31,520
Warn is suspicious.

70
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:32,886
Error is a failure.

71
00:02:33,436 --> 00:02:37,255
How many errors? Type: grep, dash C, error, app dot log.

72
00:02:37,805 --> 00:02:40,706
Grep dash C counts matching lines. Two errors.

73
00:02:41,256 --> 00:02:44,072
Show them. Type: grep, error, app dot log.

74
00:02:44,622 --> 00:02:48,078
Two failed orders: ten forty-two, and ten forty-four.

75
00:02:48,628 --> 00:02:53,919
The lines after an error matter too. Type: grep, dash A, two, error, app dot log.

76
00:02:54,469 --> 00:02:59,857
Dash A two prints two lines after each match: the stack frames the app logged.

77
00:02:59,857 --> 00:03:01,308
Checkout dot process,

78
00:03:01,308 --> 00:03:02,136
line twenty.

79
00:03:02,136 --> 00:03:04,071
The same place as the trace.

80
00:03:04,071 --> 00:03:06,074
Trace and log tell one story.

81
00:03:06,624 --> 00:03:10,293
Warnings come first. Type: grep, warn, app dot log.

82
00:03:10,843 --> 00:03:14,257
Slow price lookups. Early signals, before the failures.

83
00:03:16,307 --> 00:03:17,822
Where to look, in this order.

84
00:03:18,372 --> 00:03:22,724
First, the exit code. Zero is success. Anything else is a failure.

85
00:03:23,274 --> 00:03:27,156
Second, the log. Grep for error, count them, read the lines after.

86
00:03:27,706 --> 00:03:32,272
Third, the trace. What happened, where, which line is yours, and the last Caused by.

87
00:03:32,822 --> 00:03:34,720
Facts first. Fixes second.

88
00:03:36,770 --> 00:03:37,677
Your lab task.

89
00:03:37,677 --> 00:03:40,789
Lab eighty-one gives you a program that crashes,

90
00:03:40,789 --> 00:03:41,761
and a log file.

91
00:03:41,761 --> 00:03:43,511
Lab check judges your work.

92
00:03:44,061 --> 00:03:46,199
It lives at git hub dot com,

93
00:03:46,199 --> 00:03:47,725
slash it dash waves,

94
00:03:47,725 --> 00:03:49,481
slash dev ops dash A I,

95
00:03:49,481 --> 00:03:50,320
in academy,

96
00:03:50,320 --> 00:03:50,626
lab,

97
00:03:50,626 --> 00:03:52,381
lab eighty-one dot S H.

98
00:03:52,931 --> 00:03:55,342
One: compile checkout into the out folder.

99
00:03:55,892 --> 00:04:00,137
Two: run it, and save only the error output into crash dot text.

100
00:04:00,687 --> 00:04:04,314
Three: save every error line of the log into errors dot text.

101
00:04:04,864 --> 00:04:08,320
Four: save how many error lines there are into count dot text.

102
00:04:08,870 --> 00:04:10,576
Five: answers dot text,

103
00:04:10,576 --> 00:04:11,466
three lines.

104
00:04:11,466 --> 00:04:12,504
Top exception,

105
00:04:12,504 --> 00:04:13,320
root cause,

106
00:04:13,320 --> 00:04:14,358
and bad value.

107
00:04:14,358 --> 00:04:16,806
Read crash dot text to find them.

108
00:04:17,356 --> 00:04:17,889
Install,

109
00:04:17,889 --> 00:04:18,689
then lab up,

110
00:04:18,689 --> 00:04:19,355
lab check,

111
00:04:19,355 --> 00:04:20,221
and lab down,

112
00:04:20,221 --> 00:04:21,021
like before.

113
00:04:21,021 --> 00:04:23,820
The shortcut file is shortcuts eighty-one.

114
00:04:25,870 --> 00:04:27,939
Pass or fail. You need all six.

115
00:04:28,489 --> 00:04:30,303
Five pass lines in lab check.

116
00:04:30,853 --> 00:04:33,114
You can say what happened, from the first line.

117
00:04:33,664 --> 00:04:35,136
You can find the root cause.

118
00:04:35,686 --> 00:04:37,627
You can find your own code in a trace.

119
00:04:38,177 --> 00:04:40,204
You can find the same failure in the log.

120
00:04:40,754 --> 00:04:42,674
lab down, nothing left behind.

121
00:04:43,224 --> 00:04:44,525
To pass, all six.

122
00:04:46,575 --> 00:04:49,412
Your turn, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

123
00:04:49,962 --> 00:04:52,053
One: lab up, and read the task file.

124
00:04:52,603 --> 00:04:55,568
Two: compile and run. Save the error output.

125
00:04:56,118 --> 00:04:59,148
Three: grep the log. Save the errors and the count.

126
00:04:59,698 --> 00:05:03,346
Four: read the trace. Find the root cause and the bad value.

127
00:05:03,896 --> 00:05:06,178
Five: write your answers, and lab check.

128
00:05:06,728 --> 00:05:09,310
Six: lab down, and prove nothing is left.

129
00:05:09,860 --> 00:05:11,694
Pause the video, and begin now.

130
00:05:27,244 --> 00:05:28,119
Quick check.

131
00:05:28,669 --> 00:05:31,037
What does the first line of a stack trace tell you?

132
00:05:31,587 --> 00:05:33,208
Which line is the root cause?

133
00:05:33,758 --> 00:05:35,827
How do you find your own code in a trace?

134
00:05:36,377 --> 00:05:38,127
How do you count errors in a log?

135
00:05:38,677 --> 00:05:42,410
